WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.435
This is CNN breaking news.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:04.571
This breaking news,

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:06.740
a judge has just ordered former Vice

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.808
President Mike Pence to testify

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:10.744
before a federal grand jury.

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.912
That is related to the special counsel's

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.714
January six investigation.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.016
Let's get straight to our reporter,

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.550
CNN's Katelyn Polantz.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.786
Katelyn, what more do we know

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.322
Well, I'm just learning from a source

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:23.256
that Mike

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:24.491
Pence, the former vice

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:26.426
president, does have to testify

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.327
to the grand

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.261
jury investigating

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.062
January six

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.764
that federal grand jury

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:32.499
in Washington, D.C.

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.267
not only does he have to testify,

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.769
but he will have to testify

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:36.936
about conversations

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:39.272
he had with Donald Trump himself.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.007
Leading up to January six.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.443
So that crucial piece of information

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.045
that the Justice Department has sought

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:47.280
and that Pence has sort of divulged

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.782
in his public speeches

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:50.517
and in his book previously,

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:51.584
but that the grand jury

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:52.952
has not heard about yet

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:55.221
the conversations before January six,

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.222
when Donald Trump

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.391
and Mike Pence were on the phone,

00:00:58.391 --> 00:00:59.592
one on one.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:00.360
And Donald Trump

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.128
apparently was berating him,

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.530
calling him names,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.798
that sort of thing

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:06.332
in this criminal investigation.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.434
He is going to have to share that.

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.402
Now, we have confirmed two,

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.304
that this ruling came down yesterday

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:11.805
in the D.C.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.907
district court from the new chief judge,

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:14.741
Jeff Boasberg.

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.509
He also shot down

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.645
Donald Trump's arguments

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:19.712
that the conversation

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:20.713
should be protected

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.682
because of presidential secrecy,

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:23.683
executive privilege.

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.219
So Trump lost in this court fight.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:27.420
And Pence

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.521
does have to testify

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:29.522
about some of these things.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.057
But Pence also

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.226
got a little bit of a win himself

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.895
in that this judge decided that

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.230
Mike Pence, as the vice

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.299
president, should have some protection

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:42.435
about his around his work on January six

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.503
when he says

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:44.971
he was operating as somebody

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:46.139
as part of Congress,

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:47.373
as the vice president.

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.476
The presiding officer over the Senate.

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.643
So he doesn't necessarily

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:51.811
have to testify

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:53.313
about what happened on January

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.647
six itself.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.116
But those crucial questions

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:56.983
about what happened

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.618
before that date

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.720
and what happened between him and Trump.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.822
This judge says he is going to have

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:03.523
to share that.

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:05.258
Now, of course, he could appeal,

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:07.093
but this is a major ruling

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.229
in the special counsel investigation.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:09.762
Caitlin,

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:10.730
how much of a victory

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.065
is this for Jack Smith, the

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:12.866
special counsel?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.636
It's another victory for special counsel.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:17.237
Jack Smith.

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:18.571
He has gotten

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.607
he has locked up testimony from Mike

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:21.741
Pence's top aides.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.209
He has gotten many,

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.644
many rulings in his favor

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.411
where Donald Trump

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:28.014
has tried to block answers before this

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:28.948
grand jury.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:31.351
I mean, this is a really crucial one

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.852
because in a lot of ways,

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.287
Mike Pence is

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:36.222
one of the ultimate victims

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.724
on January six.

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.859
And so what his interactions

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:40.727
were with Trump

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:42.662
could be quite important to

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.930
a criminal case

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.765
if that is what the grand jury

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:47.700
is investigating. Right now.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.335
So, Alex, it is quite serious.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.137
It also is quite serious

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:52.172
that the courts are diving

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.072
into this question of

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.307
what is the vice president?

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.309
Does he get the protections of Congress?

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.243
And it looks like there is

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:58.178
a ruling there.

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.879
We'll have to see if the appeals court

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:00.480
firms up

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.781
any of the law around that, though.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:02.582
Alex,

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.483
you have that special counsel,

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.050
of course,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.118
also carrying out

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:06.619
a simultaneous investigation

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.855
on the classified documents

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:10.590
proceeding quietly.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.158
But but quickly, it seems,

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:13.960
and as Kaitlan just noted,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.529
has had a series of victories of late.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:17.764
I call it Caitlin Points.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:19.199
Terrific reporting.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:20.600
We'll get back to you. Thank you so much.

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:22.335
With me now is

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:23.603
CNN anchor and

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.471
senior legal analyst Laura Coates.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:26.639
And CNN

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.775
senior political analyst Gloria Borger.

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:30.877
Laura, I want to go to you first.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:32.478
Your reaction to this ruling?

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.013
I mean, it's extraordinary.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.982
We have the former vice president

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.083
in states

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.084
having to testify

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:38.585
in a grand

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:39.752
jury against the person

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.154
that he served under, the former

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:42.622
president, Donald Trump.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:43.957
Remember, he has been thinking

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.024
that he might have to do this

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:45.992
for quite some time.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:48.194
Let's delineate two things here, Alex.

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.796
On the one hand, it's his role

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:51.364
as the president of the Senate

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:52.265
and the vice president.

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:54.033
So what he's talking about on the floor

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.335
as a member of the Senate.

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:56.970
So to speak, in that capacity

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.571
is not part of this inquiry.

00:03:58.571 --> 00:03:59.606
What is relevant

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:02.508
here is what Trump may have said to him

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.277
prior to January six.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:05.211
Did he acknowledge that

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:06.546
he'd lost the election?

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:07.547
Did he seek to try

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:08.881
to block certification?

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.683
What specifically did he say?

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:11.951
Was there anything threatening?

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.119
What were the terms

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.388
that he wanted him to abide by?

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.490
What steps were actually taken

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.591
to try to disrupt

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:19.926
the peaceful transit

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:20.893
transfer of power

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.862
in our American democracy?

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.397
All of this extremely relevant

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:25.832
and yet another defeat.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:27.800
Remember, you've got former attorneys

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.702
and current attorneys of Trump

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.504
who have been asked to testify

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.940
in the grand jury, a federal grand jury.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.475
Now, you've got somebody

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:37.744
who was the second in command

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:39.045
who, of course, was threatened

00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:40.413
on January 6th,

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.848
who was a bit of a hero

00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:43.182
for so many on that day

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:44.250
for standing true.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.152
Now having to testify

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:46.919
about things

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.088
that the president said to him.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.757
This is an extraordinary moment

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:52.558
in American history.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.493
And Gloria

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.128
Pence is at an interesting position.

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:56.396
He was subpoenaed.

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:57.897
He defied the subpoena.

00:04:57.897 --> 00:05:00.800
He has at the same time, been critical

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.070
of President Donald Trump and

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.906
his actions around January six.

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:08.641
Pence, of course, was in the Capitol.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:11.177
So what was the Pence thinking here

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.980
and not in resisting having to testify

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.716
Well, in conversations that I've had,

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:17.650
I think that the

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.286
the people who are close to Pence

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.422
sort of assumed at some point

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:24.791
he would have to testify about something.

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:26.793
And as Caitlin was saying earlier,

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:28.494
he might not have to testify

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:29.462
when he was in the Senate.

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:31.731
But these conversations,

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:33.700
you know, between Donald Trump

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.068
and Mike Pence

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:36.069
before January

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:37.270
six are very important

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.339
because what they're trying to find out

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.674
is any instances

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:43.076
in which Donald Trump

00:05:43.076 --> 00:05:45.278
may have acted corruptly,

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:47.480
whether he spoke with Mike Pence

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.015
about trying to overturn

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:52.485
the election, for example, and his lawyer

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:53.052
was saying

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:54.921
whether he actually admitted

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:57.457
to his vice president at any point

00:05:57.724 --> 00:05:58.925
that he knew that

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:00.660
he had lost the election.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.995
And, you know, in Mike Pence's book,

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:05.098
he wrote about a private lunch

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:07.166
13 days after the election

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.168
in which he told Donald Trump

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:11.337
that he should just accept defeat

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:12.038
and go on

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:14.073
and think about perhaps running again.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.241
I think they're going to want

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:17.009
to flesh that out about

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:19.345
whether Donald Trump actually

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:21.848
indicated to him that he knew

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:23.983
that he had been defeated.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:26.018
So this is very, very important.

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:28.254
When you talk about the insurrection

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:30.957
and how they might want to build the case

00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:32.792
against the former president

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:34.994
and whether he incited an insurrection,

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:35.628
knowing full

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:37.764
well that he had lost an election.

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:39.665
And Laura, this does come on the

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:41.167
heels of this judge

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:43.569
also ordering

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:44.504
other top aides,

00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:45.605
including Chief of Staff Mark

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:47.206
Meadows, to testify

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.008
in this very same case.

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:49.742
Correct me if I'm wrong,

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:50.743
but I think there's a sense

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.612
that the classified documents

00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:55.014
investigation is nearing its end.

00:06:55.248 --> 00:06:55.948
While Jack

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:57.950
Smith's other investigation

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.719
into January six

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:00.753
might not be as far along,

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:01.788
but what does this tell us about

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.923
where this investigation stands?

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:05.792
Well, you can imagine almost the idea

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:07.460
of a boa constrictor

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.629
going around its particular victim

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:11.364
because it's tightening around

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:11.964
and the former

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.733
president of the United States.

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:14.700
We we're almost color

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.702
coding the various investigations

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:17.870
surrounding Donald Trump

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.105
at this point in time.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:20.840
And now you have the loss of privilege

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.475
to the executive privilege level.

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:23.876
The attorney client privilege

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:24.977
has now been

00:07:24.977 --> 00:07:26.679
pierced in a variety of ways.

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.080
And then you got this idea

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.382
of a probably more clear

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:31.651
cut case about the classified documents

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:33.352
it's a finite universe.

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:34.020
You know,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.154
when they were supposed to be

00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:35.988
handed over,

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:37.590
you know, that they were not handed over.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.725
He has admitted to not wanting to do so.

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:40.493
And it's provided

00:07:40.493 --> 00:07:42.829
some just to vacation in his mind

00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:44.063
that is not really viable

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:45.631
as to why he can retain them.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.366
That's a little bit more open and shut

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:48.434
if you handed him over

00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:49.202
or you did not,

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:50.903
either you had your attorney

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:53.005
do something nefarious or you did not.

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:55.107
The January 61, as we all know,

00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:57.243
is a much more comprehensive

00:07:57.376 --> 00:07:59.479
and complex case to look at.

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:00.012
It involves

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:00.613
whether or not

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:02.615
he gave a virtual code red.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:04.083
Was there a conspiracy?

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:05.551
Was there a seditious action

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:06.352
by a former president

00:08:06.352 --> 00:08:07.620
of the United States at the time

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:08.554
he was the president.

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.223
And so it would make sense

00:08:10.223 --> 00:08:12.391
if the Jack Smith special counsel

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:14.594
is to essentially

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:16.062
complete one investigation

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:17.230
that's far less complex

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:18.231
and then continue to

00:08:18.231 --> 00:08:19.465
build on the next one.

